[?1049h[40;1H[?1h=FUX-CORPUS(1)                                        User Commands                                       FUX-CORPUS(1)

[1mNAME[0m
     fux-corpus - recordings of real programs, replayed through terminal emulators

[1mSYNOPSIS[0m
     [1mfux-vt-compare[0m [1mrecord[0m [1m--keys[0m [4mfile[24m [1m--out[0m [4mprefix[24m [[1m--size[0m [4mrows[24mx[4mcols[24m] [1m--[0m [4mprogram[24m [[4margs[24m...]
     [1mfux-vt-compare[0m [1mcorpus[0m [[1m--engines[0m [4mlist[24m] [[4mname[24m...]

[1mDESCRIPTION[0m
     A recording is what a program wrote to its terminal, byte for byte, while keys were typed into it, one step at a
     time.  [1mrecord[0m runs the program on a pseudoterminal as a pane of [1mfux[0m(1) runs it, with [1mTERM=xterm-256color[0m and an
     environment of its own, and answers its queries as fux would.

     [1mcorpus[0m gives each recording to fux-vt and to other engines, and compares their screens after every step, cell by
     cell.

[1mOPTIONS[0m
     [1m--keys[0m [4mfile[24m
            One step a line, written as escapes: [1m\e[0m for ESC, [1m\r[0m for Return, [1m\x[0m[4mNN[24m for any other byte.  A line starting
            with [1m#[0m is a comment.

     [1m--size[0m [4mrows[24m[1mx[0m[4mcols[24m
            The terminal's size; 40 by 120 if not given.

     [1m--scrub[0m [4mold[24m[1m=[0m[4mnew[24m
            Replace [4mold[24m in the output, before it is saved.

     [1m--engines[0m [4mlist[24m
            Engine names joined by commas, as [1mrun[0m takes them.

[1mFILES[0m
     [4mcorpus/NAME.bin[24m
            The bytes the program wrote, in order.

     [4mcorpus/NAME.json[24m
            What was run, its version, the size, the environment, and each step's keys with where its output ends.

[1mEXAMPLES[0m
:[K[K     Record [1mless[0m(1) paging a file, typing [1mj[0m twice and then [1mq[0m:
:[K[K
:[K[K         printf 'j\nj\nq\n' > less.keys
         fux-vt-compare record --keys less.keys --out less -- less README.md

[1mEXIT[0m [1mSTATUS[0m
     [1mcorpus[0m exits 0 when every recording expected to agree does, 1 when one does not, and 2 on a usage error.

[1mSEE[0m [1mALSO[0m
     [1mfux[0m(1), [1mxterm[0m(1), [1mless[0m(1)

fux-vt-compare                                        2026-10-02                                         FUX-CORPUS(1)
